  78   
  79  **** Model formulation begins here:
  80   
  81  * The model formulation uses capital letters to indicate decision variables and lower-case letters to describe model parameters
  82   
  83  *===============
  84  *1. Declare sets
  85  *===============
  86   
  87  * The model divides a river network into nodes and links. Nodes are denoted by the set j. k is an aliase of j.
  88  * The links in the model are denoted by a starting node and an end node. Q(j,k), for instance, desrcibes flow (Q) between nodes j and k.
  89  * An example of a river network for the Lower Bear River, Utah that is represented in links and nodes can be found here: http://bearriverfellows.usu.edu/wash/2005/LBRNetwork.jpg
  90   
  91   
  92  SETS     s       sub-indicators. Here s1=riverine habitat s2=floodplain connectivity and s3=impounded wetlands
  93           j       river netowrk nodes which lists all nodes in the network
  94           dem(j)  demand sites which are a subset of the model nodes (j)
  95           v(j)    reservoirs which are a subset of the model nodes (j)
  96           y       The type(s) of priorty fish species that the model uses as indicator species
  97           n       The type(s) of prioty vegetation species that the model uses as indicator species
  98           t       timesteps in months
  99   
 100           RA_par_indx    indexes to read the parameters for the reservoir volume elevation curves
 101   
 102           sf_par_indx    indexes to read the parameters for stage-flow relationships
 103           wf_par_indx    indexes to read the parameters for width-flow relationships
 104           wsi_par_indx   indexes to read the parameters for wetlands suitability index relationships
 105   
 106           rsi_indx      indexes to read the parameters for riverine suitability indicator equations
 107           fci_indx      indexes to read the floodplain connectivity indicator equations
 108   
 109           MassBalanceNodes(j)      Lists all the nodes that will balance the flow not including demands sites because a diffrent equation is used to describe their mass balance
 110   
 111           run        number of runs (iterations) for the allowable shortage to build a WASH-Demand pareto-optimal curve
 112   
 113           Qrun       number of runs (iterations) for flow values to test the model sensitivity and response to differnt flow values
 114  ;
 115  *Define alias required to run the model and its loops
 116  Alias (j,k)       ;
 117  Alias (run,run2)  ;
 118  Alias (Qrun,Qrun2);
 119   
 120  *=================================
 121  *Define Parameters and Scalars
 122  *=================================
 123  PARAMETERS
 124           LinkID(j,k)             A unqie ID to every link in the network to be used for GIS visulaization
 125           linkexist(j,k)          Describes if the link from node j to node k exists (1=yes and 0=no)
 126           envSiteExist(j,k)       Describe if the link is an environmental site or not (1=yes and 0=no). Environmental sites are were sensitive habitat is located and aquatic floodplain and wetland ecological indexes are calculated
 127           returnFlowExist(dem,k)  Defines if a return flow exists on a link from a demand site back to the river network  (1=yes and 0=no)
 128           DiversionExist(k, dem)  Defines if the link is a diversion from the network to a demand site  (1=yes and 0=no)
 129           WetlandsExist(j,k)      Defines if the link has impoounded wetlands (1=yes and 0=no)
 130           LinktoReservoir(j,v)    Defines if the link flows into a reservoir or not (1=yes and 0=no)
 131           LinkOutReservoir(v,j)   Defines if the link leave a reservoir or not and carry reservoir releases (1=yes and 0=no)
 132           wght(s,j,k,t)           The model spatial and temporal weights on every sub-indicator as set by stakeholders [0: no important - 1: important]
 133           reachGain(j,t)          Defines the flows that feed a node in the network - gain could be headflow or a tributary
 134   
 135           aw(j,k,t)               Total impounded wetlands area (when filled to maximum depth) [Mm^2]
 136   
 137           lss(j,k,t)              Net losses on link j entering k  as a [%] of flow. Losses could be due to seepage to the groundwater
 138           evap(v,t)               Evaporative losses in reservoirs [m per month]
 139           linkEvap(j,k,t)         Evaporative losses on links [m per month]
 140   
 141           cons(dem,t)             Consupmtive use fraction expressed as a [%] of all inflow received at a demand site
 142           lng(j,k)                Length of river links [m]
 143           minstor(v)              Inactive reservoir storage [Mm^3]
 144           maxstor(v)              Reservoir storage capacity [Mm^3]
 145           dReq(dem,t)             Demand requirements in a loop [Mm^3 per month]
 146           dreqBase(dem,t)         Base Demand requirements at demand sites [Mm^3 per month]
 147           dcap(k,dem)             Physical capacity of diversion links [Mm^3 per month] that feed demand sites
 148           instreamReq(j,k,t)      Minimum instream flow requirement as regulated by authories or required for other purposes such as hydropower or endangered species or water rights[m3 per month]
 149           cst(n)                  Unit cost of implementing the management objective of Revegetating species n [$]
 150   
 151           RA_par(v, RA_par_indx)  Reads the reservoir area volume curves parameters
 152   
 153           sf_par(j,k,sf_par_indx) Reads the stage-flow relationships parameters
 154           wf_par(j,k,wf_par_indx) Reads the width-flow relationships parameters
 155           wsi_par(j,k,t,wsi_par_indx)   Reads the wetlands suitability index monthly relationships parameters
 156   
 157           rsi_par(j,k,t,rsi_indx)     Reads the RSI equation parameters as a function of time for differnt life stages
 158           fci_par(j,k,t,fci_indx)     Reads the FCI equation parameters as a function of time for monthly flows
 159   
 160   
 161           InitSTOR(v)             Defines inital reservoir storage [Mm^3]
 162           InitD(j,k)              Defines inital river depth for the first time step [m]
 163           InitC(j,k)              Defines inital vegetation cover for the first time step [Mm^2]
 164   
 165   
 166           qmin(j,k,t)            Defines the physical and natural lower bounds of flow on links [Mm^3 per month] as defined by the channel geometry and historical records
 167           qmax(j,k,t)            Defines the physical and natural upper bounds of flow on links [Mm^3 per month] based on headflow received and reservoir capacity upstream of every link
 168           Qsim(j,k,t)            Flow values for simulation purposes [Mm^3 per month]
 169           CMax(j,k)              Maximum vegetation cover allowed based on site floodplain area and revegetation potentials [Mm2 per month]
 170           SimLink(j,k)           Defines the links where flow is fixed for the simulation case
 171           LinkExistSim(j,k)      Defines all other links where flow is not fixed for the simulation case
 172   
 173           g(j,k,t,n)             The natural growth area [Mm2 per month] of species n
 174   
 175           DValue(run)            Define parameters to run the model over multiple values of demand requirements to build a tradeoff curve
 176   
 177           QLoop(Qrun)            Define flow values for the flow sensivity analysis loop
 178   
 179           FlowMarginal(j,t)      A parameter defined to store the shadow values of flow mass balance
 180   
 181           h(j,k,t)               A decay value to define the rate of which the aquatic suitability index curve reaches a maximum of 1
 182   
 183           maxrv(j,k,t,n)         Maximum revegetation value
 184           minrv(j,k,t,n)         Minimum revegetation value
 185     ;
 186   
 187   
 188  SCALARS
 189           b                       Total management budget to implement restoration actions [$]
 190  *Parameters for unit conversion:
 191           Sqkm_ac                 Area unit conversion square km to acre /247.11 /
 192           Mm3mo_cfs               Flow unit conversion million cubic meter per month to cubic feet per second  /13.4289/
 193           Mm3_acft                Volume unit conversion million cuic meter to acre-feet / 810.714/
 194                         ;
